{
  "id": "PPA-14",
  "title": "Root Radial Transport & Casparian Strip Barrier",
  "subtitle": "Radial nutrient uptake from soil through cortex and endodermis into ascending xylem stream",
  "caption": "Radial passage of water and essential mineral ions from the root rhizosphere through epidermis, cortex, the endodermal Casparian strip barrier, and into the ascending xylem transpirational stream (PMM-12). In outer root tissues, the plasma membrane H+-ATPases (AHA1/AHA2) generate a -160 mV potential that powers influx of nitrate (NRT1.1, NRT2.1/NAR2.1), phosphate (PHT1;1/PHT1;4), potassium (AKT1, HAK5), and iron (FRO2/IRT1). Apoplastic solutes are halted at the endodermis by the Casparian strip, an impermeable ring of lignin organized by CASP1-5 scaffolds, SGN3/GSO1 receptor kinase, and PRX64 peroxidase. Water flows transcellularly via PIP1;2/PIP2;1. In the central stele, minerals are loaded into xylem vessels by specialized efflux transporters (SKOR, PHO1, NRT1.5, BOR1).",
  "species_anchor": "arabidopsis_thaliana",
